        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Charlemagne</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 748 CE – 814 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">2 April, most likely in 748 (an older tradition gives 742); place unknown; eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrada-of-laon">Bertrada of Laon</a>, of the Frankish Carolingian dynasty</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">28 January 814, after seven days bedridden with pleurisy; buried that day in the palace chapel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> (768–814, jointly with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carloman-i">Carloman I</a> until 771); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a> (774–814); emperor (800–814)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">uniting most of Western and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>; the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>; his coronation as emperor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> in 800; the Massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a> and the forced conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a>; reforms of administration, law, coinage and the church</span></div>
