    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Charlemagne succeeded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> with his brother in 768, became sole king in 771, and by conquest added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> to his rule.</li><li>His wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> lasted over 30 years and included the beheading of a recorded 4,500 prisoners at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>, laws punishing pagan practices with death, enslavement and forced removals.</li><li>Crowned emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 800, he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, legislated on government, church and learning, and was later claimed as a predecessor by both French and German monarchs.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>As an early sign of friendship Charlemagne asked the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> for an elephant; the animal, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas">Abul-Abbas</a>, arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in 802.</p></aside>
    </section>

