    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">748</span><span class="rf-text">Born, most likely in April of this year, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrada-of-laon">Bertrada of Laon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">768</span><span class="rf-text">Succeeds his father as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> with his brother Carloman and is crowned at Noyon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">771</span><span class="rf-text">Carloman dies suddenly, leaving Charlemagne sole king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">774</span><span class="rf-text">Pavia surrenders; Charlemagne deposes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> and takes the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">782</span><span class="rf-text">After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> defeats a Frankish army, has 4,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> prisoners beheaded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>, as the annals record</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">800</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> proclaims him emperor and crowns him at mass in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">813</span><span class="rf-text">Crowns his only surviving son, Louis, as co-emperor at an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">814</span><span class="rf-text">Dies of pleurisy and is buried in the chapel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short.jpg" alt="Pepin the Short" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PippinImperialChronicleCorpusChristiCollegeMS373Fol14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">from the Middle Ages, unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Charlemagne was born into the Carolingian family, which had risen as mayors of the palace under the Merovingian kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, after whom he was named, left the throne vacant from 737 and planned the division of the kingdom between his sons Carloman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>, who succeeded him in 741. In 744 Pepin married Bertrada, a member of an influential noble family; Charlemagne was their eldest son.</p><p>His year of birth is uncertain. An older tradition, resting on his biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>, the Royal Frankish Annals and his original epitaph, gives 742. Karl Ferdinand Werner challenged this from an annal recording the birth in 747, and Matthias Becher showed that 2 April, the day commemorated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a> Abbey, would have fallen in 748, since the annalists began the year at Easter; most scholars now accept April 748. The place is unknown.</p><p>His native tongue was a Germanic language, and he was probably bilingual in Germanic and Romance dialects from an early age; he also spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The extent of his formal education is unknown, though he was almost certainly trained in military matters at his father's itinerant court. Whether he could read is debated: Johannes Fried thinks it likely, while Paul Dutton concludes that he probably never properly mastered the skill. By 751 or 752 Pepin had deposed the Merovingian Childeric III and replaced him as king. In 754 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> anointed Pepin, and Charlemagne and his younger brother Carloman were anointed with their father. He began issuing charters in his own name in 760.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/desiderius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/desiderius.jpg" alt="Desiderius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tremisse_di_Re_Desiderio,_Dominio_Longobardo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pepin died on 24 September 768, and Charlemagne and Carloman succeeded him. They were crowned separately on 9 October, Charlemagne at Noyon and Carloman at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, and kept separate palaces while being considered joint rulers of a single kingdom. They marched together against the uprising in Aquitaine, but Carloman turned back for unknown reasons and Charlemagne finished the campaign alone, capturing Duke Hunald. The episode opened a rift between the kings.</p><p>In 770 his mother Bertrada brokered a marriage alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a>, whose daughter, traditionally known as Desiderata, she brought back as his bride; Pope Stephen III wrote to both kings decrying the match. Charlemagne already had a son, Pepin, born in 769 to the Frankish noblewoman Himiltrude. Carloman died suddenly on 4 December 771, leaving Charlemagne sole king. He moved at once to secure his brother's territory, and Carloman's widow Gerberga fled with her children to Desiderius. Charlemagne ended his Lombard marriage and married Hildegard, daughter of Count Gerold, a powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> in Carloman's kingdom.</p><p>His first campaign as sole king, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, destroyed the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irminsul">Irminsul</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eresburg">Eresburg</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-i">Pope Adrian I</a> appealed for help against Desiderius, Charlemagne first offered gold and then crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to besiege Pavia in late 773. The city surrendered by June 774. He deposed Desiderius, took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> with the Lombard royal treasury.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii.jpg" alt="Pope Leo III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Leo_III_portrait_%E2%80%93_Triclinium_Leoninum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Various: Original: Unknown author Restoration: Ferdinando Fuga (1743), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Charlemagne's reign was a period of expansion. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> almost continuously for over 30 years, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> in 778, received the submission of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, deposed his cousin Duke Tassilo and absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 788, and warred against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, whose kingdom collapsed under further campaigns by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin of Italy</a>. His son Louis captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801, enlarging the Spanish March. During the wars of the 790s he established his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, where he built a large palace with a chapel.</p><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 800, at mass in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> proclaimed him "emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>" and crowned him; Leo had fled to him in 799 after his enemies attacked him. Charlemagne was the first reigning emperor in the west since the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a> in 476. Historians differ on how far he planned the coronation: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a> writes that he would not have entered the church had he known the pope's plan, while Roger Collins and Johannes Fried hold that he had intended it since 799 or 798.</p><p>The Capitulare missorum generale of 802 required all free men to swear loyalty to him and assigned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missi-dominici">missi dominici</a> in pairs, a cleric and a lay aristocrat, to administer justice in defined territories. The council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 794 set grain prices and reformed the coinage. He held 23 synods, required the Rule of St. Benedict in monasteries and promoted schools; scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodulf-of-orleans">Theodulf of Orléans</a> gathered at his court in the revival known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/widukind"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/widukind.jpg" alt="Widukind" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nig_Widukind_von_Sachsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> resisted longest. In 782 the Saxon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and defeated a Frankish army; Charlemagne compelled the Saxon magnates to hand over prisoners, and the annals record that he had 4,500 of them beheaded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. Fried writes that the figure may be exaggerated but that "the basic truth of the event is not in doubt"; Alessandro Barbero calls it "perhaps the greatest stain on his reputation". The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulatio-de-partibus-saxoniae">Capitulatio de partibus Saxoniae</a> imposed the death penalty for pagan practices. Frankish armies carried Saxon captives into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and in the 790s Charlemagne forcibly removed a large number of Saxons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>. Widukind accepted baptism in 785.</p><p>Within the family, the fate of Carloman's widow and sons after the capture of Verona is unknown; Fried suggests that they were forced into a monastery or that "an act of murder" smoothed Charlemagne's ascent. In 792 his son Pepin the Hunchback, sidelined from the succession, conspired with Bavarian nobles to assassinate his father and brothers; Pepin was sent to a monastery and many of the conspirators were executed. In 778 Basque forces had ambushed and defeated his army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Battle of Roncevaux Pass</a>.</p><p>His imperial title brought conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a> was unwilling to recognise it. Later judgements have differed sharply. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> criticised his apparent subjugation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> saw him as a despotic ruler, and German historiography either emphasised Verden, with Charlemagne as the "butcher" of the Saxons, or played it down.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa.jpg" alt="Frederick Barbarossa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_I._Barbarossa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Montecappio, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Charlemagne left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the summer of 801 and never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. From then he ruled in a more sedentary way from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and rarely led armies in person. The Divisio Regnorum of 806 set the terms of his succession: his son Charles was to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, while Pepin and Louis were confirmed in their kingdoms with additional lands; the imperial title was not addressed. He campaigned in Saxony for the last time in 804, and in 810 led a force against the Danish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudfred">Gudfred</a>, who was murdered by two of his own men before the emperor arrived; a settlement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> followed in 811. Envoys of the Byzantine emperor Michael I came to Aachen and recognised him as emperor.</p><p>In 810 and 811 his sister Gisela, his daughter Rotrude and his sons Pepin the Hunchback, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin of Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-younger">Charles the Younger</a> died, leaving the succession plan in disarray. He declared Pepin of Italy's son Bernard ruler of Italy and made Louis, his only surviving son, heir to the rest of the empire. On 11 September 813, at a large assembly at Aachen, he crowned Louis co-emperor and Bernard king.</p><p>He fell ill in the autumn of 813 and spent his last months praying, fasting and studying the gospels. He developed pleurisy, was bedridden for seven days and died on the morning of 28 January 814. His daughters and palace officials buried him that day in the chapel at Aachen. Louis arrived 30 days later and took charge of the palace and the empire. The remains were exhumed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> in 1165 and reinterred by Frederick II in 1215.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule.jpg" alt="Carolingian minuscule" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule">Carolingian minuscule</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carolina-muster-sw.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CRolker, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The stability of the reign did not long outlive him. Louis faced rebellions by his own sons, and after his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> divided the empire into West, East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> ruled, with interruptions, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> until 911 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> until 987. The East Frankish king Otto the Great was crowned emperor in 962, founding or re-establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, which lasted until 1806. The coronation of 800 also began a centuries-long ideological conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> known as the problem of two emperors.</p><p>Scriptoria produced an estimated 90,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the 9th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule">Carolingian minuscule</a> script influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and modern typefaces. Thomas F. X. Noble writes that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> "as a historical phenomenon" is "a Carolingian construction". Antipope Paschal III elevated Charlemagne to sainthood in 1165 at Barbarossa's urging, but the Holy See did not recognise him as a saint.</p><p>Over 1,000 legends are recorded about him, according to Matthias Becher, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Roland and other works of the Matter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> made him a leader of Christian knights against Muslims. French and German monarchs both counted him as "Charles I"; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> viewed him as the first constitutional monarch, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and Hitler each made use of his legacy. Historiography after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> has focused on him as "the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>", a view first advanced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a>, and since 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> has awarded a prize in his honour to those who promote European unity.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Charlemagne (748–814), eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>, became king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 768, conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and was crowned emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 800. His reforms and the learning of his court outlasted his empire, which his grandsons divided, and later ages claimed him as the &quot;father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>&quot;.</em></p>

